  "notes": "A pop-R&B-Latin hybrid built on hip-hop rhythm beds, modelled on the half-time engine the research names: \"the hip-hop-leaning singles feel **half-time** (backbeat on 3) around 92–101\", so `halfTime` is true and the snare is `halftime_3`. The 120–128 club band is the other backbeat class and belongs to the club engine, so it is named here rather than encoded. Three drum facts drive the block — \"kick on 1 and the '&' of 2\", \"16th hat, tambourine on the off-8ths\" (the tambourine lane with `placement` \"offbeat\"), and \"timbale/conga overlay on the Latin-facing tracks\", which is the rest of the perc block. The low end is \"sub-heavy synth bass doubling the sample's bass, portamento slides between roots on the R&B material\" — a synth bass, so no `bass808` block is authored and the inherited null keeps the bassline generator alive; the slides are `glideProb` 0.3. Harmony is \"a sampled or interpolated 2- or 4-bar harmonic cell\" with \"little functional cadence\", which is `maxChords` 3, i–VI–VII and i–iv, and a vamp-leaning harmonic rhythm. The topline is \"deliberately low-range and repetitive\" with the background stack \"doing the harmonic lifting\", hence a low register, a low density and a counter at a high ratio. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research says so and marks the tempo values **[LOW-CONF]**. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are all drum probabilities and `bpm.mode` 96.",
